hod":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":false,"statement":"","confidence":0},"academicOrientation":{"score":50,"confidence":0.45},"profileConfidence":0.22,"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"0299ac2638a683dfb70604099191006ca778b6fce967a190e46d8250907a0749","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Society & Culture","secondarySubjects":["Politics & Government"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book examines the societal implications and political contexts of atrocities and suffering, aligning it primarily with Society & Culture and secondarily with Politics & Government.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"86b8f30ada88a41500de434867764128ddfaa2cc49304d5ea1ce21d4ee6fa974","summary":"Blocking out, turning a blind eye, shutting off, not wanting to know, wearing blinkers, seeing what we want to see ... these are all expressions of 'denial'. Alcoholics who refuse to recognize their condition, people who brush aside suspicions of their partner's infidelity, the wife who doesn't notice that her husband is abusing their daughter - are supposedly 'in denial'. Governments deny their responsibility for atrocities, and plan them to achieve 'maximum deniability'. Truth Commissions try to overcome the suppression and denial of past horrors. Bystander nations deny their responsibility to intervene. Do these phenomena have anything in common? When we deny, are we aware of what we are doing or is this an unconscious defence mechanism to protect us from unwelcome truths? Can there be cultures of denial? How do organizations like Amnesty and Oxfam try to overcome the public's apparent indifference to distant suffering and cruelty? Is denial always so bad - or do we need positive illusions to retain our sanity? States of Denial is the first comprehensive study of both the personal and political ways in which uncomfortable realities are avoided and evaded. It ranges from clinical studies of depression, to media images of suffering, to explanations of the 'passive bystander' and 'compassion fatigue'.","pageCount":360,"publicationYear":2001,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Social Science","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-states-of-denial-knowing-about-atrocities-and-suffering-stanley-cohen"}],"displaySummary":"When we deny, are we aware of what we are doing or is this an unconscious defence mechanism to protect us from unwelcome truths? Can there be cultures of denial? How do organizations like Amnesty and Oxfam try to overcome the public's apparent indifference to distant suffering and cruelty? Is denial always so bad - or do we need positive illusions to retain our sanity? States of Denial is the first comprehensive study of both the personal and political ways in which uncomfortable realities are avoided and evaded. It ranges from clinical studies of depression, to media images of suffering, to explanations of the 'passive bystander' and 'compassion fatigue'.","primaryTopic":"Intellectual Biography","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Society & Culture","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Society & Culture","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Society & Culture","mappedSubject":"Society & Culture","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book examines the societal implications and political contexts of atrocities and suffering, aligning it primarily with Society & Culture and secondarily with Politics & Government.","id":"subject-evidence-book-states-of-denial-knowing-about-atrocities-and-suffering-stanley-cohen-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"Social Science","mappedSubject":"Society & Culture","score":5,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-stat